{"subjectId":"python","id":"scope-closures","title":"Scope & Closures","group":"Functions","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Python resolves names with the LEGB rule, and inner functions can <em>close over</em> variables from the function that created them.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>LEGB: where a name is looked up</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Local</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Names assigned inside the current function.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Enclosing</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Locals of any enclosing (outer) functions.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Global</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Names at the top level of the module.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Built-in</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>len</code>, <code>print</code>, <code>range</code>, … from the <code>builtins</code> module.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>x = \"global\"\n\ndef outer():\n    x = \"enclosing\"\n    def inner():\n        return x              # found in the enclosing scope\n    return inner()\n\nprint(outer(), x)             # enclosing global</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-world\"></i><code>global</code> and <code>nonlocal</code></h3>\n<p>Assigning to a name makes it local to that function. To rebind an outer name, declare it first.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>count = 0\n\ndef bump():\n    global count\n    count += 1\n\ndef make_counter():\n    n = 0\n    def inc():\n        nonlocal n            # rebinding the enclosing variable\n        n += 1\n        return n\n    return inc\n\nbump(); bump()\nc = make_counter()\nc(); c()\nprint(count, c())             # 2 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Without <code>nonlocal</code>, <code>n += 1</code> inside <code>inc</code> raises <code>UnboundLocalError</code>: the assignment makes <code>n</code> local, so reading it before assigning fails.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-access\"></i>Closures</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Closure</strong> — a function object that remembers variables from the scope where it was defined, even after that scope has finished running.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def multiplier(k):\n    def times(x):\n        return x * k          # k is captured\n    return times\n\ndouble, triple = multiplier(2), multiplier(3)\nprint(double(5), triple(5))                       # 10 15\nprint(double.__closure__[0].cell_contents)        # 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Late binding in loops</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Every lambda sees the final value of i:\nfns = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]\nprint([f() for f in fns])          # [2, 2, 2]\n\n# A default argument captures each value at definition time:\nfns = [lambda i=i: i for i in range(3)]\nprint([f() for f in fns])          # [0, 1, 2]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Closures capture <em>variables</em>, not values. The value is read when the function is <strong>called</strong>.</p></div></section>"}